e for your  sample application running in kubernetes  engine the fourth URL takes you to the  load generation tool use it to simulate  20 users with a hatch rate of 2 which  means that 2 users will be added every  few seconds until we get up to the full  20 now that your application is under  load you can use stack driver to observe  it start by viewing the kubernetes  cluster by selecting kubernetes engine  from the resources menu in the cloud  console you can use this dashboard to  see the performance of your  infrastructure workloads and services  and you can click on each row in a  dashboard to get metrics and logs for  that resource because  this is a distributed application  composed of multiple microservices  understanding service latency is key  click on trace on the left side and then  go to the trace list to see the request  being traced then click on a request to  see how it traverses the services in the  application and latency for each service  call  finally to understand the performance  and resource utilization of your  services themselves go to profiler  select the product catalog service and  CPU time is the metric profiler shows  you CPU utilization down to the method  level and you can use this data to  optimize both service performance and  resource utilization we've built the  stack driver sandbox to give you an  opportunity to try these and other style  features quickly and easily we'll come  back to it in future episodes and use it  to learn about features like error  reporting and debugger thank you for  joining me today  and come back soon for more this is a  stack dr. stay healthy out there  [Music]
 

